WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.460
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday, October 30th.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.800
You know, Hank, sometimes people will accuse me
of not being a "real" nerd

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.800
because, you know, I don't read that much 
science fiction, I like sports a lot...

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.280
So I want to thank you for sharing the book
I wrote when I was eight, "It Just Isn't Fair,"

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.940
because it proves once and for all
that I am definitely a nerd.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.540
Hank, can we just look at the cover art
of that book real quickly?

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:30.420
Okay, here we go.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.220
On the left there, you see me—
for some reason I'm kind of a quasi-abstract painting—

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:38.980
and then over there on the right, saying "wimpy, wimpy," you see a popular person.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.800
Hank, here's what gets me about "wimpy, wimpy" guy.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.560
"Wimpy, wimpy" guy is obviously my eight-year-old understanding of what a popular person is.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.820
And that means that when I was eight,

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:52.060
I thought that "cool" was wearing mismatched plaid pants hiked up to your solar plexus.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.459
Hank, I was such a nerd
that I thought the nerds were cool.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.820
Hank, the reason you couldn't find my box
full of childhood treasures in North Carolina

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.480
is that a few years ago,
Mom and Dad sent it to my house.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.140
Where it is kept safely under lock and key
from the prying eyes of you and your minions.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.200
That said, I would like to show you some highlights
from *my* childhood box.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:13.210
First we have this family portrait from 1981, which is a totally normal family portrait in every single way...

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.920
except that I clearly need to pee.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.260
I also have a report about Thomas Edison
I wrote in third grade—

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.659
Thomas Edison, you know,
who's famous for inventing the boom box.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.600
I don't want to brag, Hank,
but I did get an A+ on the report,

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.540
even though I used the word "interesting" eleven times.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.320
That's the great thing about third grade, Hank:

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.500
If you've got one polysyllabic adjective,
everyone thinks you're a genius.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.309
Hank, it's not that I think Mom and Dad
*made* me massively unpopular in elementary school,

00:01:36.309 --> 00:01:38.740
but sometimes I'll come across
something from my childhood

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.740
and I'll be like, "Mom and Dad, what were you thinking?"

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.319
Like, for instance, say I wanted a sticker album.

00:01:44.319 --> 00:01:49.028
I think that's great. Get me a sticker album for my birthday, by all means. Sticker albums are awesome.

00:01:49.639 --> 00:01:51.100
Don't make it pink!

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.869
Although to be fair, while Mom and Dad picked out the album itself, I picked out all the rainbow stickers.

00:01:55.939 --> 00:01:57.620
God, I love rainbows.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:01.520
Hank, my favorite thing about the pink stickers album is it's got like a page for like, googly-eyed stickers,

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.560
and a page for my favorite stickers,
and a page for scratch-and-sniff stickers

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.260
—and then it's got a page for military insignia stickers.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:08.960
Weird!

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.440
Also in my childhood box we have this beanie.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.120
Now Hank, I wish I could tell you that Mom and Dad bought me this beanie when I was in third grade

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.580
but the plain fact of the matter is that I bought it using my own money when I was a sophomore in high school.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.820
Hank, the final thing I'd like to show you,
unfortunately I don't have.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:26.020
I don't know *where* it is.
It may be in North Carolina, actually.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.200
I'm referring, of course, to the only trophy I ever won.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.860
Hank, it was 1984, and I was still in tee-ball,

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.460
even though everyone else in my class had moved on to the Minor Leagues of Little League.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:38.220
Hank, I think you might have been spared Little League baseball, so let me give you some quick terminology.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.900
Tee-ball is when instead of hitting a pitch that's thrown at you, you hit a ball that's just sitting on a tee.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.860
So, because I was like four years older than all the other tee-ball players, I was relatively good.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.100
I mean, I was terrible, but I was just better
than most of the five-year-olds.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.780
And so at the end of the season
they announced the all-star team, and...

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:54.380
I didn't make the all-star team.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.700
I was beat out by a nice kid named Bryce,
who was, I think, four.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.480
But out of pity they named me an alternate
for the all-star team,

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.150
and the all-star game itself was occurring
over Thanksgiving weekend,

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.370
and I didn't want to go see Grandpa in Indiana,

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.560
because I was worried that Bryce might get injured
and my services might be needed in the all-star game.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.140
Also I was kind of bummed out
because only the actual all-stars got trophies;

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.620
the alternates only got, like, certificates or something.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.790
So, partly to convince me to leave town for Thanksgiving and partly because they're nice people and they love me,

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.460
Mom and Dad had a trophy made for me.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:28.660
And the trophy says "John Green, 1984, all-star... in our hearts."

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:33.500
[Groan]

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.340
I'm an all-star in their hearts, Hank!

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.560
And that's the only trophy I ever won as a child.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.779
So those are my nerd credentials, Hank.

00:03:39.779 --> 00:03:40.870
-Tchk tchk, pow!-

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.440
I think I've proven my point. I'll see you tomorrow.

